.po 1i
.TH UMASK 1C UCB
.V 08/15/84 17:08:36
.\" @[$]umask.1C	1.7 08/15/84 17:08:36 - Zilog Inc.
.ad
.fi
.SH NAME
umask \- set file-creation mode mask
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBumask\fR [\fInnn\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The user file-creation mode mask is set to
.IR nnn .
The three octal "digits" in \fInnn\fR
refer to \fIread\fR/\fIwrite\fR/\fIexecute\fR
permissions for \fIowner\fR, \fIgroup\fR, and \fIothers\fR,
respectively (see
.BR chmod (2)
and
.BR umask (2)).
The value of each specified "digit" in \fInnn\fR is subtracted from the
corresponding "digit" in the file creation mode (for example, 777 for 
executable files, 666 for data files) specified by the system
(see \fIcreat\fR(2)).
.PP
If
.I nnn\^
is omitted, the current value of the mask is printed.
.PP
.B Umask\^
is recognized and executed by the shell.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
% umask 000

% cat /dev/null > umask.00

% ls -l umask.000
-rw-rw-rw- 1 deck          0 Mar 13 12:23 umask.000
.fi
.PP
The other umask values are as follows:
.sp
.nf
-rw-rw-rw- 1 deck          0 Mar 13 12:23 umask.111
-r--r--r-- 1 deck          0 Mar 13 12:23 umask.222
-r--r--r-- 1 deck          0 Mar 13 12:23 umask.333
--w--w--w- 1 deck          0 Mar 13 12:23 umask.444
--w--w--w- 1 deck          0 Mar 13 12:23 umask.555
---------- 1 deck          0 Mar 13 12:23 umask.666
---------- 1 deck          0 Mar 13 12:23 umask.777
.fi
.PP
If two digits are specified, as in:
.sp
.RS
% umask 22
.RE
.sp
The first (owner) permission bits are untouched and the
second and third fields are affected as in
the following file:
.sp
.RS
-rw-r--r-- 1 deck          0 Mar 13 12:23 umask.22
.RE
.sp
.PP
If only a single number is specified, it affects the
last field (the "others" permission bits) as in the command:
.sp
.RS
% umask 2
.RE
.sp
The following file permission bits result:
.sp
.RS
-rw-rw-r-- 1 deck          0 Mar 13 12:23 umask.2
.RE
.sp
.PP
In other words, 
.B "umask 022"
removes
.I group\^
and
.I others\^
write permission (files normally created with mode
.B 777
become mode
.BR 755 ;
files created with mode
.B 666
become mode
.BR 644 ).
.SH SEE ALSO
chmod(1),
sh(1),
chmod(2),
chown(1),
creat(2),
umask(2),
chmog(M).
.br
\fIThe C Shell\fR in the ZEUS Utilities Manual


